# Firebase input field notes

Whenever you create or change a page/form that collects user input (text, select, checkbox, textarea, file, coordinates, etc.):

1. Update `f:\KeirosPhase2All\Firebase-Dataset-Inputs.md` in the same turn.
2. Add or revise a section for that page with:
   - Page name
   - Collection / entity suggestion (short snake or camel name)
   - Each input: field key, label, type, required?, notes/enums
3. Do not wait until the end of the project — keep the note current as UIs are built.
4. Filters/search used only for UI filtering can be listed under “UI-only filters” (not persisted), or omitted if ephemeral.
5. Persistable create/edit/settings fields must always be listed.

## Entity / correlation rules (keep in sync)

Canonical schema: `ERP_Full/src/data/schema.ts` · join helpers: `ERP_Full/src/data/entityMap.ts` · overview: Entity model section in `Firebase-Dataset-Inputs.md`.

When documenting fields:

1. Prefer **ID refs** (`propertyId`, `organizationId`, `floorId`, `locationId`, `mapVersionId`, `routeId`, `propertyCodeId`, `apiClientId`, `roleId`, …) over display names.
2. If a UI shows a name (`propertyName`, `org`, `mapper`), note it as a **cached label**; store the FK for Firebase.
3. Ops entities (codes, routes, versions, sessions, mapping queue, analytics) join through **`SiteProperty`** via `propertyId` (complex id or independent building id).
4. If you change the entity graph or add a new FK, update the **Entity model (joins)** section in `Firebase-Dataset-Inputs.md` in the same turn.

Do not invent Firebase schemas beyond what the UI collects; this note is the source for later dataset design.
